  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "code_only",
      "classification": "validated",
      "note": "The cached object contains exact CUDA PTX and its contract. Every policy calls the same original march and owns its context, host arrays, model, FFT plans and graph. The only replaced global is the runtime constructor."
    },
    {
      "id": "strict_provenance",
      "classification": "validated",
      "note": "Embedded hashes check corruption. The frozen protocol independently binds artifact bytes. The diagnostic runtime compares the full artifact SHA to the frozen expectation before driver module load, and missing, corrupt, digest mismatch, load failure or policy mismatch does not fall back."
    },
    {
      "id": "compiler",
      "classification": "scope",
      "note": "The 11.8 version, two compiler file digests, exact source, filename, entries, target and ordered flags bind the key. The diagnostic validates matching disk compiler, cuFFT and driver identities before and after. The loaded Windows dependency origin was not separately enumerated by this audit. PTX is virtual device code, so driver module loading and possible JIT remain."
    },
    {
      "id": "atomic_lock",
      "classification": "validated",
      "note": "Per-key thread lock and Windows byte-range lock protect cooperative readers and writers. Writers close a flushed and fsynced same-directory temporary before atomic replacement. Saved fixtures cover thread/process deduplication, owner exit, corrupt payload, simulated failed replacement, exact repair and key changes."
    },
    {
      "id": "strict_directory",
      "classification": "scope",
      "note": "Strict mode opens a+b on the sidecar lock. It leaves artifact bytes unchanged but can create a lock file and requires a writable lock directory. A frozen archive is immutable by hash, not necessarily readable on a physically read-only mount with the current loader."
    },
    {
      "id": "fixture_coverage",
      "classification": "scope",
      "note": "The saved synthetic suite has no explicit missing-key fixture or internally self-consistent modified PTX/full-digest rejection fixture. Static behavior is fail closed; the latter additionally needs an independent artifact expectation as the GPU diagnostic provides."
    },
    {
      "id": "comparison_fairness",
      "classification": "scope",
      "note": "FD176 dense and FD184 band share the same key but each gets its own disabled and verified-hit solve with the matched observer and unchanged schedule. T9 contains no Legendre trajectory. A module recipe shared with Legendre is no substitute for method-specific T72 numerical qualification."
    },
    {
      "id": "clock_accounting",
      "classification": "scope",
      "note": "The T9 outer diagnostic starts before owned launch setup and ends at a child timestamp, excluding model release after the result is returned, signature and serialization. It is distinct from the formal parent-return clock. Per-module CPU compile/read times do not include an entire all-key prebuild command. Charge preparation once per distinct key, disclose workload and amortization, and preserve empty-first total cost."
    },
    {
      "id": "driver_environment",
      "classification": "scope",
      "note": "The registered diagnostic uses default driver cache settings. Future freezes with a nondefault path would need to bind its identity without exposing private path text because the current nondefault_path value records only presence. A shared default driver cache can still affect order in this unisolated pilot."
    },
    {
      "id": "no_performance_claim",
      "classification": "scope",
      "note": "This audit reads the frozen plan and CPU evidence only, never timed a solve and does not authorize a promotion or an isolated speed claim."
    }
  ],
